    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d295">

                <pb facs="0404" n="336" xml:id="pg336"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72/2352½a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        President Wilson
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-01-17">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 17, 1916</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: I enclose for your
                    consideration the draft of a letter to the British Ambassador dealing with the
                    submarine question, and suggesting a method by which future loss of life on
                    merchant vessels might be avoided.<anchor n="8" xml:id="d295-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>My first inclination was to send letters to the German Ambassador and Austrian
                    Chargé, but two reasons prevented; first, I was convinced that the German and
                    Austrian Governments would assent to the proposal as it only required them to
                    conform to the rules of international law, while it required their enemies to
                    modify a present practice which might be construed into the relinquishment of a
                    legal right; and, second, if Germany and Austria acceded promptly to the
                    suggestion, any demur by Great Britain, France, Italy or Belgium would, if it
                    became known (as it would undoubtedly through the German or Austrian Embassies),
                    arouse adverse criticism in the press of this country and excite public
                    resentment against the Entente Powers, which appears to be increasing from day
                    to day.</p>
                <p>By adopting this method of approach the proposal can be kept secret if it is
                    refused by the Entente Governments and if it is considered inexpedient to make
                    it public.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d295-f1" n="8" xml:id="d295-f1-content">
                <p>For text of the letter as sent, see <ref target="frus1916Supp#pg_146">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1916, supp., p.
